WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.020
Pointer APIs should communicate whether null is allowed, whether data may be modified, and how long the pointer remains valid.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:13.400
If a parameter is required and cannot be null, a reference is often clearer than a raw pointer.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.620
The type tells the caller that an object must exist.

4
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:25.040
If missing data is a valid case, use an explicit optional pattern or document that nullptr is allowed and what it means.

5
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.600
Unclear nullability creates inconsistent checks.

6
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:34.860
One caller may validate before calling, another may rely on the callee, and a third may assume the value is always present.

7
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:43.920
At trust boundaries, such as public APIs, plugin interfaces, parsers, or C interop layers, validate pointer inputs defensively.

8
00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:50.900
Inside well-defined internal code, prefer contracts that make repeated defensive guessing unnecessary.

9
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.580
Constness is another part of the contract.

10
00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:58.320
A pointer to const data communicates that the pointee should not be modified through that access path.

11
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:04.100
A const pointer communicates that the pointer value itself should not be reseated.

12
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.640
These meanings are different, and reviewers need the distinction.

13
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:16.220
Clear const use helps separate read-only observation from mutation, which is especially important when data is shared across components or threads.

14
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.900
Good pointer APIs are narrow.

15
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.200
They avoid accepting null when null is not meaningful.

16
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.980
They avoid mutable access when only inspection is needed.

17
00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.840
They describe ownership and lifetime instead of relying on habit.

18
00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:36.500
Callers should not need to reverse-engineer whether a pointer is an optional input, required output, or mutable view.

19
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.200
When the answer matters for safety, encode it in the interface.

20
00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:43.420
The safer interface makes misuse stand out during review.
